A||||13||BC-GT||Best way to clean used ashtrays?||||1||
Z||000000||BC-GT||08-07-2001||07:12 PM||||Got a couple of ashtrays at the boneyard today but they are filthy. Don't smoke myself but would like to have them in the car anyway. Them things are hard to find for some reason!<P>------------------<BR>Darryl Martin<BR>1985 GT <BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.imagestation.com/picture/pf251a969c96964ef15691e97e7ac4489/feb64e3a.jpg.orig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||DXR_DAD||08-07-2001||08:03 PM||||Sand blast! Quick and easy<P><BR>DXR_DAD||||reg||3
Z||000002||starfighter007@msn.com||08-07-2001||08:28 PM||||yuk!! If you dont have access to a sand blaster, try soaking in soap and water over night scrub a little rinse and repeat.GRRROOOSe!!goodluck||||reg||9
Z||000003||Bruce||08-07-2001||08:43 PM||||Easier just to buy a couple of new ones from the Fiero Store; just a few dollars a piece, as I remember.||||reg||1
Z||000004||fieronv||08-07-2001||10:09 PM||||After soaking over night in soapy water use "Mother's" aluminum polish and a dremel tool with a small buffer pad. It not only cleans them but make them look like they are chromed.||||reg||1
Z||000005||PBJ||08-07-2001||10:37 PM||||clean'm and paint'm black??||||reg||1
Z||000006||Bluebottlejunky||08-07-2001||11:19 PM||||Um I used CLR..and then polished up with metal wax..<P>------------------<BR>Hello, My name is Mike and I am addicted too fiero's...PLEASE dont help me.<BR>85'GT 4sp<BR>AIM: Fierofanatic2001||||reg||1
Z||000007||Neal||08-08-2001||12:42 AM||||steel wool works wonders, but it will leave a scratched finish.<P>metal tarnish remover may work as well||||reg||1
Z||000008||Songman||08-08-2001||01:26 AM||||Spideyfoot did a thing with his that makes them look new.. not black but that aluminum color.. maybe he'll see this thread..<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
Z||000009||Oreif||08-08-2001||09:45 AM||||I used a dremel tool with rubbing compund and Mother's polish.<BR>Click on the link below for the original thread and pics.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Archives/Archive-000002/HTML/20001019-1-001789.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Archives/Archive-000002/HTML/20001019-1-001789.html</A> <P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg">    <BR><B><I>Got Emblems?</I></B>||||reg||1
Z||000010||BC-GT||08-11-2001||10:34 PM||||Hey Oreif, those ashtrays you did with the Dremel tool look outstanding. Looked at some Dremels briefly today but there is lots of them, any one in particular I should be looking for (like cordless maybe, certain RPM, etc etc) thanks.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000011||Oreif||08-12-2001||05:15 AM||||The one I used was a variable speed one. You don't want one that just has a high speed. Either get a variable speed or one that has a high and low. You need the low speed to apply the polish.||||reg||1
Z||000012||theogre||08-12-2001||02:39 PM||||Variable speed corded unit is the only way to go. Cordless tools suck. Unless you use them all the time the batteries go to hell in them.<P>I've had a single speed one for years. they are nice but you really need to slow them down for some tools. Slower speed is important for cutoff wheels polishing and brush tools. They last/work much better at lower speeds.||||reg||1
Z||000013||spideyfoot||08-12-2001||04:15 PM||||For that new factory look <BR>A. Sandblast<BR>B. basecoat with Grey Primer lrt dry<BR>C. Shoot a layer of silver/bumper chrome and wipe off with a clean rag real quick like.<BR>This somehow combines over the primer and gives it that factory new look without being too silvery. ||||reg||1
